{
  "format": "sfxgen/1",
  "name": "Bullet fly-by",
  "description": "You are being shot at across open ground and the round only just misses your head: snap-zip, then, much later, the gun. The bullet is faster than its own sound, so the first thing you hear is its shock wave slapping your ear, a sharp dry crack, the thing that makes you duck. Then the zip of the round itself tearing away to the right, a ragged buzz inside a hiss of torn air (the spinning slug, never a clean whistle), its pitch falling as it goes, gone almost at once. Much later the rifle's own report arrives from far off: a dull, low, pitchless thump with its top end eaten by the air, and a short roll off the ground and the tree line.",
  "tags": [
    "weapon",
    "bullet",
    "flyby",
    "whizz",
    "doppler",
    "gun",
    "near miss",
    "pass",
    "crack",
    "war"
  ],
  "duration": "auto",
  "seed": 29,
  "output": {
    "channels": 2
  },
